## 1. Introduction
Skin, as the major exposed organ of the body, is prominently prone to injury and microbial invasion [1,2]. Generally, injuries pertaining to skin result in two types of wounds: acute and chronic, and much where the latter requires longer timescales to self-repair [3]. Apart from the numerous factors that affect the wound healing, the associated health issue that runs in parallel is becoming a major global health concern [4]. According to a global survey, ${\sim}17$ million victims die from bacterial infection annually [5]. One of the major culprits in infections associated with a chronic wound is microorganisms surrounding the environment, as they readily invade and grow on the wounded site. The formed bacterial colonies would then penetrate into deeper layers of the tissue and hinder the healing process [2].
Wound dressings are commonly used to cover the injured site to prevent bacterial infection. Ideal merits of wound dressings include marked biocompatibility, ease of removal, good permeability, and absorbability of tissue exudates [6]. Among various dressings, nanofibrous membranes (NFMs) have become one of the contested candidates due to their microstructure that mimics extracellular matrix (ECM) [7,8], which attracts fibroblasts and accelerates tissue repair. Polyvinyl alcohol (PVA) NFMs have advantages of excellent film-forming and biocompatibility, which are appealing in wound dressing applications [9]. However, the abundant hydroxyl groups on the PVA give rise to hydrolysis, leading it to suffer from instability in aqueous solution. Besides, the inadequate antibacterial activity for PVA NFMs limits its application as an advanced wound dressing [10]. Therefore, designing insoluble PVA-based wound dressing with anti-infection ability is of great interest and importance.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are crystalline hybrid porous solid structures comprising of metal nodes and organic linker, and have attracted increasing attention in the applications of gas storage, separation, catalysis, filtration and sensing in recent years [11–15]. MOFs with porous structure possess large specific surface areas, and combine the advantages of ease of synthesis and high tenability, thus acting as an suitable candidate for biomedical applications [4,16–18]. Particularly, benefiting from the high and regular porosity, MOFs cumulate the merits of high drug loading and controlled release as drug delivery systems [19–21]. However, their utilization in above applications is limited due to their brittle nature in the powder phase [12,22]. As reported in literatures, MOF-substrate integration is a robust strategy to extend the exceptional properties of MOFs, including, but not limited to, the growth MOF layers on substrates and incorporation of MOF crystals into mixed matrix membrane [23–26]. For example, Yuan et al. have successfully constructed MOF/cotton composite by coating zincbased zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF) onto gauze which is subsequently used for the application of microbicidal wound dressing [16]. This practical effectiveness offers a win-win opportunity in MOF/substrate composites design. Inspired by this, MOF-based PVA membranes will address concerns related to mechanical stability of standalone MOFs and enhance the mechanical strength of pristine polymer membranes [27]. Moreover, introducing additional MOFs on PVA membranes provides a feasible strategy to improve the stability of PVA NFMs in the presence of water. Among available MOFs, the $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ is proved to be chemical stable and is a viable candidate as the base platform for building a functional system [28–31]. In particular, MOF crystals act as an ideal drug carrier for antimicrobial compounds, where active drug components are encapsulated or entrapped inside its inherent pores or attached to its surface by electrostatic and hydrophobic interactions [19]. For example, Lv et al. constructed an efficient drug carrier system based on UiO-66- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and loaded linezolid and lysozyme via electrostatic adsorptions and molecular interactions for antibacterial application [30]. To our knowledge, only few studies have been performed on UiO$66\substack{-\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ MOF-based PVA NFMs for insoluble and antibacterial wound dressing.
In this work, we propose an in-situ seeding method, to grow UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ MOFs for producing non-soluble PVA NFMs with superior antibacterial activity. The $\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ MOFs not only act as drug carriers for LV release but also enhance practicability of PVA NFMs as wound dressings in moist environment. The morphologies of MOF-based PVA NFMs were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM), and the dynamic wetting behavior of individual membrane was visualized by a high-speed camera. The antimicrobial activity of the UiO-66- $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}}&{{}}\end{array}$ and LV loaded UiO-66- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ $\mathrm{(LV@UiO–66–NH_{2}@P V A)}$ was tested against Escherichia coli (E. coli) and Staphylococcus aureus (S. aureus). Finally, the UiO-6 $\mathsf{S-N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ and $\mathrm{LV@UiO–66–NH_{2}@P V A}$ NFMs were evaluated for the treatment of S. aureus infected wounds in mice.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
Polyvinyl alcohol (PVA, molecular weight $\left(\mathrm{M}_{\mathrm{w}}\right){\sim}205,000\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mol}$ , Aldrich, $98\%$ , zirconium chloride $\mathrm{(}\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ Alfa Aesar, $299.5\%$ , 2- amino- $p$ -terephthalic acid (2-ATA, Acros Organics, $99\%$ ), $N$ Ndimethylformamide (DMF, Fisher, $\geq99.5\%$ , anhydrous ethanol and levofloxacin (LV, TCI, $98.0\%$ were purchased from commercial sources and used without further treatment.
## 2.2. Preparation of MOF-based NFMs
The preparation process of PVA NFMs was presented in the previous study [10]. In brief, the PVA aqueous solution with a solid content of $10\mathrm{{wt\%}}$ was prepared in deionized water at $80^{\circ}C$ for electrospinning. An electric potential of $20\mathrm{kV}$ was applied and flow rate was set at $1.0\mathrm{mL/h}$ . The electrospun PVA nanofibers were deposited on aluminum foil positioned $12\mathrm{cm}$ away from the syringe tip. After that, the resulting PVA NFMs were dried in vacuum for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ at $80^{\circ}C$ and then immersed in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF. A homogeneous DMF solution with equimolar concentrations of $\mathrm{\zr{Cl_{4}}}$ $(0.080\mathrm{g},0.343\mathrm{mmol})$ and 2-ATA $(0.062\mathrm{g},0.343\mathrm{mmol})$ was added into the above solution as illustrated in Fig. 1. The hybrid solution was kept at $85^{\circ}C$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ for the growth of UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ MOFs. In particular, $15\mathrm{mg}$ of LV was added along with $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ and 2-ATA into the DMF solution to prepare $\mathrm{LV@UiO–66–NH_{2}@P V A}.$ Finally, the asfabricated swatches were washed 3 times by DMF and ethanol, respectively, to remove any residual MOFs or precursors and were heated in vacuum at $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$ overnight. Besides, UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ and LV loaded UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ $\mathrm{(LV@UiO-66-NH_{2})}$ MOFs were additionally prepared for comparison.
## 2.3. Characterization
The morphologies of the pristine PVA and MOF-based PVA NFMs were characterized by Hitachi S-4800 field emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM). The PVA and MOF-based PVA NFMs were characterized on a Nicolet 6700 FT-IR spectroscopy and the spectra were recorded over the wavenumber range of $500{-}4000\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The crystalline phase analysis of MOF-based PVA NFMs was performed by X-ray diffraction (XRD) using a D/max-II B (Shimadzu, Japan) XRD tool (Cu Ka X-ray source). The tensile property was investigated using a tensile tester (XQ-1C, China) at a constant speed of $2\mathrm{mm}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ and the swatch was cut into the width size of $2\mathrm{mm}$ . The dynamic water spreading property was characterized using a contact angle goniometer (Kino SL200B, United States).
## 2.4. In vitro cytotoxicity
The cytotoxicity of MOF-based PVA NFMs was evaluated by MTS assay using L929 fibroblast cells. The NFMs were pretreated, sterilized and transferred into a 24-well culture plate [32]. The cells were seeded into each well at an appropriate number of 30,000 cells per well. The swatches were then incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in $5\%\thinspace{\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ for 24 and $48\mathrm{h}$ , respectively. After that, the MTS solution was added and incubated for another $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ for in vitro cytotoxicity.
## 2.5. In vitro antibacterial activity
The antibacterial properties of MOF-based PVA NFMs were investigated against strains of S. aureus and E. coli. The bacterial suspensions were diluted to the ${\tt O D}_{600}$ value at $0.24\pm0.01$ [33]. The UiO-66- $\left.-\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{PV}\mathsf{A}\right.$ NFMs at a concentration of $50\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL}$ and $\mathrm{LV@UiO–66–NH_{2}@P V A}$ at a concentration of 50 and $100\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ were added to the suspensions. The suspensions were shaken at $150\mathrm{rpm}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The absorbance of bacterial suspensions $(200\upmu\mathrm{L})$ at $600\mathrm{nm}$ was measured after $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Besides, the suspension was diluted with PBS for different times, and $1~\mathrm{mL}$ bacterial solution was spread onto Luria-Bertani (LB) agar plate and incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ to form viable colony units (CFU). The log reduction in bacterial population was calculated using Eq. (1).
log reduction $1=\log\mathrm{A}-\log\mathrm{B}$
Here A represents the bacterial colony units in the control group, and B represents bacteria colony units in the sample group.
## 2.6. In vivo evaluation of wound healing
The wound healing performance of swatches was investigated using a mouse model (C57BL/6, $22{\cdot}25{\mathrm{g}}$ , male). The fullthickness circular wounds with a diameter of $8\mathrm{mm}$ were created and injected with S. aureus suspension $50\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , $1.0\times10^{7}\mathrm{CFU/mL})$ for 1 h [34]. Subsequently, MOF-based PVA NFMs were utilized to cover the wounds. For comparison, non-injected and injected wounds covered by gauze were used as a blank and control group, respectively. The dressings were changed every 2 days. The wound healing process was evaluated by optical photographs, and wound size reduction was calculated. For histological analysis, skins including the entire wound and adjacent normal skin were harvested and then fixed in $4\%$ buffered paraformaldehyde. The skins were embedded in paraffin and stained for hematoxylin&eosin (H&E) and CD31. The experiments were approved and done in accordance with protocols approved by the experimental animal center of Donghua University, Shanghai, China. All care and handling of the animals were performed with the approval of Institutional Authority for Laboratory Animal Care.
## 2.7. Statistical analysis
All data were obtained at least three repeated experiments and expressed as the average value $\pm$ standard deviation.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Preparation of UiO-66-NH2@PVA NFMs
The UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ MOFs were formed through in-situ heterogeneous nucleation on the PVA NFMs as illustrated in Fig. 1. The electrospun PVA NFMs were directly immersed in DMF solution containing $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ MOF precursors to obtain UiO-66- $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}}&{{}}\end{array}$ NFMs (Fig. 1a). The embedded linker served as an anchoring point or nucleation site for the MOF growth, and the uniform yellowish color confirmed full coverage of MOF crystals on NFMs. In particular, LV, as a hydrophilic and broad-spectrum antibacterial agent acting against different types of Grampositive and Gram-negative bacteria, was pre-dissolved in the synthesis solution for preparing LV-loaded UiO-66 $\mathsf{j}{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ NFMs (labeled as $\mathrm{LV@UiO–66–NH_{2}@P V A}$ , Fig. 1b). In this way, UiO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ worked as a drug delivery system for biomedical applications. The XRD patterns in Fig. 2a showed main characteristic peaks $7.45^{\circ}$ and $8.6^{\circ}$ ) of UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}\right.$ NFM and LV@UiO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ NFM, and it was found the marked peaks were a match to those of pure UiO- $66\substack{-\Delta\mathsf{N}}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ powder [35,36]. The FT-IR spectra of PVA, UiO-6 $\mathsf{S-N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ and $\mathrm{LV@UiO–66–NH_{2}@P V A}$ NFMs were shown in Fig. 2b. Besides, the FT-IR spectra of LV, UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{L V@U i O–66\mathrm{-}N H_{2}}$ were also investigated and presented in Fig. S1. As shown in Fig. 2b and Fig. S1, the characteristic peaks at 1571 and $1657\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were attributed to the asymmetric and symmetric vibrational stretching of ${\mathsf{C O O}}^{-}$ bonds in the organic linker of $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ [30], which were also presented in the spectra of $\mathsf{L V@U i O–66\mathrm{-}N H_{2}}$ , UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}\right.$ and $\mathsf{L V@U i O–66\mathrm{-}N H_{2}}$ , indicating the successful synthesis of UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and stable structure after LV loading [37]. In addition, some small peaks ascribed to LV were also found in the spectrum of $\mathsf{L V@U i O–66\mathrm{-}N H_{2}}$ (Fig. S1). The
FT-IR results in Fig. 2b showed the bands in the region 2847– $2980{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ which corresponded to $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ stretching in the PVA molecules, and the broader band in the region $3100{-}3700\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was attributed to the hydrogen bonded hydroxyl groups, indicating the possible formation of intramolecular and intermolecular hydrogen bonding among the PVA chains owing to the strong hydrophilic force [38]. However, the spread of this broader peak around the $3100{-}3700\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ region was reduced in that of MOFbased PVA NFMs, which indicated the weakening of hydrogen bonds between the PVA chains upon its modification. The FT-IR results indicated the in-situ nucleation of MOFs that changed the surface polarity which to influence the overall water solubility.
The dynamic wetting behavior of UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}\right.$ NFMs was studied as seen in Fig. 2d. It is well known that pristine PVA membranes are readily dissolved in water. By comparison, the MOFbased PVA NFMs produced in this work were much stable under water infiltration after being decorated with UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ MOFs as the water drop was rapidly adsorbed and spread on the UiO$66\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ NFMs in ${\sim}1s$ . The enhanced mechanical property and water stability of the obtained UiO- $66{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ NFMs promise a greater potential for biomedical applications in moist environments.
## 3.3. Morphologies of MOF-based NFMs
## 3.2. Mechanical and hydrophilic properties of UiO-66-NH2@PVA NFMs
The tensile properties of the pristine PVA, UiO-66- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ and $\mathrm{LV@UiO-66-NH_{2}@P V A}$ NFMs were investigated as shown in Fig. 2c. The average tensile breaking strength and break elongation of pristine PVA membranes were $23.4~\mathrm{cN}$ and $41.5\%$ , respectively. After being decorated with $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ MOFs, the UiO-66- $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}}&{{}}\end{array}$ NFMs were mechanically reinforced, showing an average tensile strength of $73.33~\mathrm{cN}.$ , as the UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}\right.$ NFMs became thicker compared to the pristine PVA. Actually, the UiO$66\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ NFMs were measured at a thickness of ${\sim}0.4\ \mathrm{mm}$ while the pristine PVA NFMs had a thickness of ${\sim}0.1\ \mathrm{mm}$ . However, the break elongation showed a negative trend of $5.8\%$ Besides, the $\mathrm{LV@UiO–66–NH_{2}@P V A}$ NFMs had similar tensile strength and break elongation with those of UiO-6 $\mathsf{\Pi}_{[6-\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}}$ NFMs. Studies have shown that loading micro- or nano- particles improve the overall mechanical properties and a close relationship is established between the MOF loadings and the mechanical properties [27,34]. Briefly, the enhanced tensile strength of MOF-based NFMs is attributed to the interfacial interactions between the MOFs and membranes. It can be argued that the mobility of the PVA chains was restrained, making their stretching more difficult.
SEM images show the pristine PVA NFMs with a porous structure have smooth fiber-like surfaces with a diameter ${\sim}500\mathrm{nm}$ Fig. 3a and Fig. S2. After being decorated with UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ MOFs, a layer of UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was tightly patterned on the surface of PVA NFMs without changing the porous structure as shown in Fig. 3b. Fig. 3c clearly showed that UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ exhibited an octahedral microcrystalline structure with an average diameter of around $100\mathrm{nm}$ [14,39,40]; the diameter of a single UiO-66- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ fiber was estimated to be around $800\mathrm{nm}$ . As shown in Fig. 3d&e, compared with UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ MOFs on PVA NFMs, the morphology of LV loaded UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ crystals became obtuse which was attributed to the accumulated LV molecules on the MOF surface via electrostatic attraction. Besides that, it is worth noting that the original straight fibers were more sensitive to stretch and bend for both UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}\right.$ and $\mathrm{LV@UiO-66-NH_{2}@P V A}$ NFMs. We hypothesized the stabilization of PVA NFMs against water is accomplished by protection of hydroxyl groups and heat treatment [41,42]. Then we had investigated the morphologies of PVA fibers treated in $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ solution without linkers $(\mathrm{PV}\mathsf{A@Z r C l_{4}}.$ ). As shown in Fig. 3f and Fig. S3, the $\mathsf{P V A@Z r C l_{4}}$ fibers became swollen and the diameter of individual fiber was relatively larger compared with pristine PVA fibers; what’s more, the fibers deformed in this condition from a straight line to winding curl. Therefore, a proposed forming mechanism is depicted in Fig. 4a. On one hand, the reaction is initiated once the PVA fibers are immersed in $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}/D M F}$ . As reaction proceeds, $\scriptstyle{Z\Gamma^{4^{+}}}$ ions accumulate on the surface of PVA NFMs and the reaction mechanism proceeds via fast coordination kinetics between ${\cal Z}\mathrm{r}^{4+}$ and the hydroxyl groups from PVA. On the other hand, as temperature increases, the amorphous region of PVA chain partly turns into crystallization [43], and PVA fibers get deformed and shrunken, leading the diameter of $\mathsf{P V A@Z r C l_{4}}$ fibers to be larger. For the above reasons, the final product, $\mathsf{P V A@Z r C l_{4}}$ NFM armed by ${\cal Z}\mathrm{r}^{4+}$ on the surface is not found to be watersoluble but hydrophilic (Fig. S4). Similarly, when the 2-ATA linkers encounter $Z\mathrm{r}^{4+}$ in the near surface of PVA fibers at an elevated temperature, UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ crystals are gradually nucleated and grown on PVA fibers via coordination reaction as illustrated in Fig. 4b. With large amounts of amino groups from UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ MOFs being exposed on the UiO-6 $6\mathrm{-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}@\mathrm{PV}\$ surface, UiO- $66\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ NFMs exhibit a hydrophilic effect which is also confirmed in Fig. 2d.
## 3.4. In vitro cytotoxicity and antimicrobial activity evaluation
The cytotoxicity of the MOF-based PVA NFMs was evaluated using an MTS assay. As shown in Fig. 5a, the cell viability of UiO$66\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ NFMs was higher than that of LV@UiO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ , which showed a decreasing trend on the second day. The cell viability of UiO-66 $\mathsf{j-N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ and $\mathrm{LV@UiO–66–NH_{2}@P V A}$ after 24 and $48\mathrm{h}$ was 92.6 and 84.5, 86.4, and $72.3\%$ , respectively. Quantitative analysis revealed that $\mathrm{LV@UiO–66–NH_{2}@P V A}$ exhibited higher cytotoxicity than UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}\right.$ . Previous studies had proved the UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ MOFs were bio-safe at a broad concentration and there was no significant difference even at a concentration of $800\mathrm{mg/L}$ [30]. Based on the results, we infer that the mild cytotoxicity of the LV@UiO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ comes from the release of LV.
The antimicrobial activity of the MOF-based PVA NFMs was evaluated against Gram-negative E. coli and Gram-positive S. aureus, respectively. As shown in Fig. 5b, the UiO- ${\mathsf{S6-N H}}_{2}@\operatorname{PV}\mathsf{A}$ NFMs exhibited no significant antibacterial effect at the concentration of $50~\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL}$ , whereas $\mathrm{LV@UiO–66–NH_{2}@P V A}$ NFMs showed a dosedependent antibacterial effect. It could be seen that LV@UiO-66- $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}}&{{}}\end{array}$ achieved higher than $3\log$ CFU reduction of E. coli and S. aureus. This corresponds to a promising efficacy of higher than $99.9\%$ at $100\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ . Fig. 5c&d showed the photographs of agar plates after $24\mathrm{h}$ . It was found that the number of colonies was significantly decreased in $\mathrm{LV@UiO-66-NH_{2}@P V A}$ group compared with the UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{PV}\mathrm{A}$ Besides, the bacterial viability was investigated by a live/dead assay. There were large amounts of viable cells stained in green (seen left) and most was visible in red (seen right) for $\mathrm{LV@UiO-66-NH_{2}@P V A}$ group. The antimicrobial activity results in this study were further compared with the previous literatures as shown in Table S1 in the Supporting Information. Consistent with the previous results [30,44], UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ MOFs as well as UiO-6 $_{\mathsf{6-N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}}$ were proved to have a limited antibacterial effect. To endow the ability, various methods including incorporating antibacterial materials [45], photodynamic therapy [30] and chemical modification [44] could be utilized to meet different requirements in practical applications. The FT-IR results of the UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}\right.$ and $\mathrm{LV@UiO–66–NH_{2}@P V A}$ NFMs after the antibacterial application were investigated as shown in Fig. S5 in the Supporting Information. The FT-IR results showed that the characteristic peaks from UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ had no significant change after application. However, some peaks of LV molecules around 1000 and $3000{-}2750\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were disappearing after application, confirming the completion of drug release process.
The cytotoxicity and antimicrobial results both confirmed the UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ MOFs acted as an effective delivery system. Actually, the MOF/NFM based delivery system works through several ways to achieve the antimicrobial goal. On one hand, some LV is directly stored in the pores of NFMs, and the micrometer-scaled pores in the NFM scaffolds are large enough to allow drug to release rapidly, leading to the burst drug release in the early stage. In a second way, a large amount of LV is trapped into the pores of MOFs during the formation of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ . The more stable storage provides a long-term antibacterial effect. Besides, a few LV molecules attached to the MOF surface via electrostatic attraction. As shown in our previous studies, drugs would be released rapidly by direct loading into the network with large pore size which would cause potential cytotoxicity [34]. Through various delivery approaches, LV molecules can be released in a more sustainable way, enabling more effective antibacterial ability compared with the single delivery system.
## 3.5. In vivo wound healing performance
The wound healing property was evaluated on mice using an infected full-thickness wound model. LV with broad-spectrum antibacterial ability was selected as drug model which was proved to be effective in S. aureus infected wound healing [33,46]. In this test, the infected wounds were introduced to each mouse on the rare side by inoculating S. aureus bacterial suspensions, and the infected sites were covered with gauze (control), UiO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V A}$ and $\mathrm{LV@UiO-66-NH_{2}\textcircled{\4}P V A}.$ An uninfected wound covered with gauze was evaluated as the blank group. Fig. 6a showed the representative photographs of the healing process. After three days, the wound of blank group was swollen and had a red color in its appearance; the wound of control group exhibited wrinkles, and was bleeding caused by a second damage when changing the gauze. In contrast to the control group, the size of wound area in
MOF-based PVA groups had gradually decreased and there was no scar and bleeding. Besides, the wound tissues of LV@UiO-66- $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}}&{{}}\end{array}$ and control groups were collected to determine the number of bacteria after three days. As shown in the insets of Fig. 6b, it was observed the bacterial colonies in the LV@UiO-66- $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}}&{{}}\end{array}$ groups were significantly less than the control group (log reduction: $4.2\pm0.8$ vs. $0.3\pm0.1$ ). After seven days of treatment, the wounds in blank and control groups started to recover. Secretions were still observed at the surface of the wound in control group, and the wounds were found to be much larger than those treated with MOF-based PVA NFMs. The wound size was measured and the wound area reduction was quantitatively illustrated in Fig. 6b. These results indicate that the wound healing process is accelerated by treating with the MOF-based PVA NFMs, and $\mathrm{LV@UiO–66–NH_{2}@P V A}$ is a good candidate material for epithelialization and anti-infection.
Images of hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) stained wounds after three, seven and 14 days of treatment are shown in Fig. 6c. The epidermis structure was not visible and neutrophils could be observed (as the arrow showed) in the first week. As time proceeded, the granulation tissue of each wound gradually thickened. After 14 days of treatment, the epidermal layer was well-structured and hierarchical structure composed of epidermis and dermis gradually formed for UiO-66 $\mathsf{i-N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ and LV@UiO-66- $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}}&{{}}\end{array}$ NFM groups. These histological results were consistent with the wound appearances shown in Fig. 6a&b. The in vivo studies indicate that accelerated wound healing is achieved by the application of the MOF-based PVA NFMs, especially the LV@UiO$66\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ NFM group. To further examine the wound healing process of UiO-66- $\mathbf{\partial}\cdot\mathbf{NH}_{2}@\operatorname{PVA}$ and $\mathrm{LV@UiO–66–NH_{2}@P V A}$ NFM groups, the ability of vascularization by the fluorescence expression of vascular endothelial-specific marker CD31 was investigated. As shown in Fig. 6d, new blood vessels could be observed by the third day, and more of them were visible in the wound site after 14 days. It is obvious that the wound in LV@UiO-66- $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}}&{{}}\end{array}$ NFM group had a better quality of vascularization than that of UiO-66 $\mathsf{-N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ NFM group in a time of seven days. We believe that the MOF-based PVA NFMs exhibit debridement function and generate an anti-bacterial growth due to their excellent hydrophilicity. Additionally, the microporous structure of MOFbased PVA NFMs is advantageous for attracting fibroblasts as wound dressings. Particularly, $\mathrm{LV@UiO–66–NH_{2}@P V A}$ NFMs preformed a long-term antibacterial function. As a result, the MOFbased PVA NFMs provide an appropriate wound healing microenvironment for accelerating chronic wound healing.
## 4. Conclusions
On the basis of the previously reported preparative approaches [37,38], this work proposed a combined strategy for improving the stability of PVA membrane to extend its biomedical applications in moist environment. The $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ MOFs were grown in-situ on the surface of PVA nanofibrous membranes, endowing the NFMs a number of properties as wound dressings. After MOF decoration, the UiO-6 $\mathsf{S-N H}_{2}@\mathsf{P V}\mathsf{A}$ NFMs were rendered nonsoluble, but still hydrophilic. The UiO-66 $\leftrightsquigarrow\mathrm{H}_{2}@\mathrm{PV}$ NFMs with a MOF-patterned layer exhibited highly porous structure suitable for gas exchanges and exudates adsorption. Applications such as a drug carrier system were illustrated by preloading LV in the MOF precursors to prepare $\mathrm{LV@UiO–66–NH_{2}@P V A.}$ The obtained $\mathrm{LV@UiO–66–NH_{2}@P V A}$ NFMs are shown to be bactericidal with the efficiency $>99.9\%$ at $100~\mathrm{\upmug/mL}$ against E. coli and S. aureus. The $\mathrm{LV@UiO–66–NH_{2}@P V A}$ performed faster wound closure kinetics compared with a gauze dressing. Additionally, improved healing quality was observed from the histological analysis of skin tissue. The results proved the water-stable MOF-based PVA NFMs exhibited superior wound healing properties. The MOF-based PVA NFMs combined with the constructing methodology provides a feasible strategy for preparing water-stable PVA-based substrates to extend the application in aqueous environment. A further study on inducing directional cell migration is under way by the topological structure design of MOF-based NFMs so that more desirable and faster wound healing process can be gained.
